{
  "raw": {
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537257859": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English/Creative Writing",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Assistant Professor of English/Creative Writing Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Creative Writing, English, MFA Industry Higher Education, Faculty, Academic Employee Type Exempt FT Required Degree Masters Description St e p h en s Colle g e invites applicants for the position of Assistant Professor of English / Creative Writing. Join a vibrant literary community dedicated to developing and empowering women’s voices through creative writing and literary scholarship. The program seeks an engaging educator with a deep commitment to undergraduate learning and the development of the creative and professional capacities of writers at all levels. Students in the program develop their voice through engaging courses and experiences, including a passion project, a senior capstone, and the publication of an annual literary magazine, Harbinger . The successful c a n d id a t e will be able to teach a broad range of courses, including creative writing workshops, genre courses, early and/or late literature, as well as introductory courses and those serving general education. Candidates with expertise in Women’s Studies may be given preference. Esse n t i a l D u ties & Responsibilities Teach creative writing workshops and classes that may include fiction, creative nonfiction, memoir, poetry, playwriting, etc. Teaching load is 12 credit hours per semester. Teach courses in literature, including genre, early and/or late literature. Contribute to programmatic revision, course development, and ongoing curricular innovation. Collaborate with other English/Creative Writing and design faculty on shared student experiences in ways that are mutually supportive. Serve as academic advisor to English/Creative Writing students. Assist with student recruitment initiatives, in collaboration with Marketing, Admissions, Student Success & Engagement, faculty colleagues, and the Dean of the Women’s College. Service to the program, College, and profession. Requirements Required Qualifications · Doctorate or MFA in Creative Writing, Literature, English, or an allied field. · Evidence of commitment to undergraduate education, innovative course design, and mentorship. · Strong record of creative accomplishments and commitment to further creative output, including mentorship of students. · Experience teaching at the collegiate level. Preferred Qualifications · Experience in the following areas of creative writing and literature: fiction and/or script writing; early literature. · Experience teaching in small college settings, Women’s Colleges, or other mission-driven institutions. · Ability and desire to teach courses in the Women’s Studies minor. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes a comprehensive health and medical package, paid time off, an Employee Assistance Program, 403b retirement with employer contribution, tuition waivers and tuition eligibility for undergraduate classes for dependents. Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537253634": {
      "title": "Residence Hall Director (Live-On)",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Residence Hall Director (Live-On) Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Residential Life, Student Engagement, Student Residence Hall Industry Higher Education, Student Success, Residential Life Employee Type Exempt FT Required Degree 4 Year Degree Manage Others Yes Description The Hall Director is a full-time, live-on professional responsible for fostering a safe, supportive, engaging, and developmentally focused residential community at Stephens College. The Hall Director provides leadership for the day-to-day operation of the residence halls while promoting student belonging, personal responsibility, leadership development, academic success, and persistence. As a member of the Student Success & Engagement team, the Hall Director develops meaningful relationships with students, identifies and responds to student needs, supervises and develops residential student staff, supports community standards and conduct processes, coordinates residence hall operations, and collaborates with campus partners to promote student success. The Hall Director resides in College-provided housing and participates in an on-call rotation that may include evenings, weekends, holidays, College breaks, and emergency response. Essential Duties and Responsibilities 1. Residential Community & Student Success · Develop and maintain welcoming residential communities that promote belonging, connection, academic success, personal growth, and student well-being. · Establish a visible and accessible presence within the residence halls and build positive relationships with residents. · Identify students experiencing academic, personal, social, or transitional challenges and connect them with appropriate campus resources. · Collaborate with Student Success & Engagement colleagues and other campus partners to support students experiencing barriers to success. · Support new-student transition and help residents develop skills for successful independent and community living. · Contribute to College efforts related to student persistence and retention. 2. Staff Supervision & Development · Provide direct supervision, coaching, professional development, and evaluation for the Graduate Assistant Hall Director. · Recruit, select, train, supervise, and evaluate Resident Assistants and other assigned student employees. · Conduct regular individual and team meetings with residential staff. · Develop and facilitate comprehensive fall, mid-year, and ongoing training for residential student staff. · Establish clear performance expectations and provide timely feedback, accountability, recognition, and support. · Develop student staff members as leaders, peer educators, community builders, and student-success partners. · Coordinate staff schedules and ensure appropriate residence hall coverage. 3. Community Standards & Student Conduct · Educate residents and staff regarding College policies, residential community standards, student rights, and responsibilities. · Respond to alleged violations of College and Residence Life policies. · Review incident reports and conduct appropriate follow-up. · Conduct student conduct meetings and assign educational or developmental outcomes within designated authority. · Maintain accurate, timely, and confidential conduct documentation. · Coach residential staff regarding documentation, policy enforcement, confrontation, and accountability. · Promote an approach to accountability that balances student development, community safety, consistency, and individual responsibility. · Refer significant or complex conduct matters to the appropriate College administrator. 4. Crisis Response & Student Support · Participate in the professional Residence Life on-call rotation and provide response to emergencies, crises, and significant student concerns. · Provide guidance and support to Resident Assistants and the Assistant Hall Director responding to incidents. · Coordinate as appropriate with Campus Safety, Student Success & Engagement, Facilities, and other College personnel during emergencies. · Assess situations and determine when matters require escalation to senior College administrators or emergency services. · Provide timely follow-up after significant incidents. · Support College emergency response procedures, safety initiatives, and student welfare efforts. · Participate in Behavioral Intervention Team and student-support processes as assigned and appropriate to the position. 5. Residence Hall Operations · Coordinate the day-to-day operation of assigned residence halls. · Oversee residence hall opening, closing, break, and transition processes. · Assist with room assignments, room changes, occupancy records, check-in/check-out, and related housing processes. · Coordinate with Facilities regarding maintenance, custodial, building condition, and repair concerns. · Conduct routine building walkthroughs and health and safety inspections. · Oversee key/access procedures and support residence hall safety and security practices. · Provide leadership for front desk operations, including supervision and training of student employees as assigned. · Maintain appropriate records, reports, inventories, and operational documentation. · Assist with Residence Life purchasing and budget management as assigned. 6. Leadership, Engagement & Assessment · Develop and oversee residential engagement strategies that promote belonging, community, leadership, and student development. · Coach Resident Assistants in developing intentional community-building experiences rather than relying solely on event-based programming. · Create opportunities for residents to develop leadership skills and participate meaningfully in the residential community. · Collaborate with Student Engagement, Leadership Development, Career Services, the Student Success Center, and other College areas on programs and initiatives. · Establish and track Residence Life outcomes and key performance indicators. · Collect and analyze data related to residential engagement, programming, conduct, student concerns/referrals, staff performance, facilities trends, and other identified measures. · Prepare regular reports identifying accomplishments, trends, challenges, and opportunities for improvement. · Participate in Student Success & Engagement and College committees, meetings, programs, and initiatives. · Perform other duties consistent with the responsibilities of the position. Requirements Minimum Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree required. Master’s degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Counseling, Education, Leadership, or a related field preferred. Experience: Previous experience in residence life, student affairs, student leadership, or a related college environment required. Prior experience as a Resident Assistant, Graduate Assistant, Hall Director, Residence Director, or comparable role strongly preferred. Supervisory or student-staff leadership experience preferred. Skills: Strong interpersonal, communication, organizational, problem-solving, and leadership skills. Ability to develop positive relationships with students while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries and accountability. Demonstrated ability to respond calmly and effectively to challenging, sensitive, or emergency situations. Position Requirements This is a live-on position. The Hall Director must reside in College-provided housing as a condition of employment. The position requires regular evening and occasional weekend work and participation in an on-call rotation that may include holidays and College breaks. The Hall Director must be able to respond promptly to incidents occurring within the residential community. Residence Life Philosophy Residence Life at Stephens College is an integral part of the student success experience, creating communities where students develop belonging, independence, leadership, personal responsibility, and the skills necessary to thrive at Stephens and beyond. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes a comprehensive health and medical package, paid time off, an Employee Assistance Program, 403b retirement with employer contribution, tuition waivers and tuition eligibility for undergraduate classes for dependents. Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537250055": {
      "title": "Human Resources Generalist",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Human Resources Generalist Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Base Pay $50,000.00 - $55,000.00 / Year Job Category Human Resources, Administrative Support, Onboarding Industry Human Resources, Higher Education, Operations Employee Type Exempt FT Description The HR Generalist provides broad administrative and operational support across the Human Resources department, with responsibility for employee records, HRIS data integrity, onboarding documentation, compliance documentation, employee service, payroll and benefits assistance, and general HR operations. Requirements HR Operations and Administrative Support · Provide day-to-day administrative and operational support to the Human Resources department. · Respond to routine employee questions and direct employees to appropriate HR resources. · Support department workflows, calendars, checklists, recurring deadlines, and HR projects. · Prepare correspondence, forms, reports, and documentation as assigned. · Assist with HR communications, employee notices, newsletters, and department updates. · Support employee engagement activities, recognition efforts, HR events, and culture-related initiatives. · Maintain organized HR records and assist with improving HR processes and procedures. · Perform other related duties as assigned to support HR operations. HRIS, Records, and Employee File Maintenance · Maintain human resource records, including both paper and electronic employee files. · Upload personnel forms and employment documents into the HRIS system. · Enter and update employee data, including new hires, transfers, terminations, pay changes, job classification changes, and other employment updates. · Prepare and maintain new employee files, termination files, and other personnel documentation. · Conduct routine audits of HRIS and employee records for accuracy and completeness. · Support regulatory compliance and reduce litigation risk through accurate and timely documentation. · Ensure personnel records are organized, current, confidential, and maintained according to HR standards. · Assist with secure retention, retrieval, and disposal of HR records in accordance with policy. Onboarding and New Hire Documentation Support · Support the onboarding process for new employees, including preparation, tracking, and completion of required documentation. · Ensure all required new employee paperwork is submitted to HR and uploaded into the HRIS system. · Assist with onboarding checklists, new hire forms, payroll forms, benefit forms, tax forms, and direct deposit documentation. · Maintain I-9 compliance documentation and assist with completion, tracking, and retention of I-9 forms. · Support background check, drug screening, and reference check documentation processes. · Assist with new employee orientation logistics and preparation of orientation materials. · Coordinate with the HR Business Partner, HR Generalist/Payroll Specialist, hiring managers, and HR Director to assist with various HR initiatives and projects · Help ensure new employees understand key HR processes, deadlines, required forms, and available resources. Required Skills/Abilities: · Excellent verbal and written communication skills. · Excellent interpersonal, negotiation, and conflict resolution skills. · Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail. · Excellent time management skills with a proven ability to meet deadlines. · Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. · Ability to prioritize tasks and to delegate them when appropriate. · Ability to act with integrity, professionalism, and confidentiality. · Thorough knowledge of employment-related laws and regulations. · Proficient with Microsoft Office Suite or related software. · Proficiency with or the ability to quickly learn the organization’s HRIS and talent management systems. Education and Experience: · Bachelor’s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, or related field required. · At least one year of human resource management experience preferred. · SHRM-CP a plus. Physical Requirements: · Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. · Must be able to lift 15 pounds at times. · Must be able to access and navigate each department at the organization’s facilities. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537251075": {
      "title": "Director of Human Resources",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Director of Human Resources Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 East Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Higher Education, Human Resources Industry Higher Education, Human Resources Employee Type Exempt FT Description The Director of Human Resources is a member of the College’s administrative leadership team and serves as a strategic and operational partner in all areas of human resources. Working closely with the Executive Vice President – Finance & Operations, President, Cabinet, faculty, and staff, the Director provides leadership for employee relations, talent acquisition, performance management, compensation and benefits, HR policies and procedures, compliance, professional development, and organizational effectiveness. The ideal candidate will bring a combination of strategic perspective, sound HR expertise, strong interpersonal skills, and a willingness to be actively engaged in the day-to-day work of a small institution. The Director will help create a workplace culture in which employees feel valued, supported, accountable, and connected to the College’s mission. Essential Responsibilities Human Resources Leadership ¦ Serve as a senior HR advisor to college leadership, administrators, managers, faculty, and staff. ¦ Translate institutional priorities into effective HR strategies, programs, policies, and practices. ¦ Partner with the EVP and senior leaders on workforce planning and organizational development. ¦ Help establish consistent, transparent, and service-oriented HR practices across the College. ¦ Represent Human Resources on institutional committees and initiatives as assigned. Employee Relations ¦ Provide guidance and coaching to managers and employees regarding workplace concerns, performance issues, conflict resolution, and employment practices. ¦ Investigate employee relations matters and recommend appropriate actions in consultation with college leadership and legal counsel when appropriate. ¦ Promote a positive, respectful, inclusive, and mission-centered workplace. ¦ Help managers develop effective approaches to communication, feedback, accountability, and employee development. ¦ Maintain appropriate confidentiality and documentation regarding sensitive personnel matters. Talent Acquisition and Onboarding ¦ Lead or oversee recruitment processes for staff and administrative positions. ¦ Partner with hiring managers to develop position descriptions, recruitment strategies, interview processes, and selection criteria. ¦ Promote effective and equitable hiring practices. ¦ Strengthen onboarding and orientation programs to help new employees become engaged and productive members of the College community. ¦ Develop strategies that support employee retention and workforce stability. Performance Management and Development ¦ Oversee and continuously improve the College’s performance management processes. ¦ Support managers in establishing clear expectations and providing meaningful feedback. ¦ Develop and coordinate professional development and leadership-development opportunities. ¦ Help identify opportunities for employee growth, career development, and succession planning. ¦ Promote a culture of continuous learning and accountability. Compensation and Benefits ¦ Assist with the administration and ongoing evaluation of compensation programs. ¦ Support periodic review of salaries, job classifications, and position responsibilities. ¦ Help oversee employee benefits programs and relationships with benefits providers. ¦ Communicate benefits information effectively and accurately to employees. ¦ Provide executive oversight and have ultimate accountability of the organization’s payroll function, ensuring payroll is processed accurately, timely, and in compliance with applicable federal, state, and local laws, regulations, and organizational policies. HR Compliance and Policy ¦ Help ensure compliance with applicable federal and Missouri employment laws and regulations. ¦ Maintain and periodically review employee handbooks, policies, procedures, and HR forms. ¦ Monitor regulatory developments and recommend policy or procedural changes when necessary. ¦ Maintain appropriate personnel records and documentation. ¦ Partner with legal counsel and College leadership on employment-related compliance and risk-management matters. HR Operations and Systems ¦ Oversee effective day-to-day HR operations and customer service. ¦ Ensure HR processes and systems are accurate, efficient, and appropriately confidential. ¦ Maintain and improve HR information systems and reporting capabilities. ¦ Develop workforce reports and metrics to support institutional decision-making. ¦ Supervise, mentor, and develop HR staff as assigned. ¦ Assist in preparation and management of the Human Resources budget. Requirements ¦ Bachelor’s degree in human resources, business administration, organizational leadership, or a related field. ¦ At least seven years of progressively responsible human resources experience, including leadership or supervisory experience. ¦ Demonstrated experience in employee relations, recruitment, performance management, HR compliance, and HR operations. ¦ Working knowledge of federal and state employment laws and regulations. ¦ Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, and organizational skills. ¦ Demonstrated ability to handle confidential and sensitive matters with discretion and sound judgment. ¦ Ability to build effective working relationships with employees at all levels of an organization. Preferred ¦ Master’s degree in human resources, business administration, higher education administration, organizational development, or a related field. ¦ SHRM-SCP, SPHR, or comparable professional certification. ¦ Experience working in higher education. ¦ Experience working in a small college, university, nonprofit, or other mission-driven organization. ¦ Experience administering faculty contracts. ¦ Experience with HRIS implementation or optimization. ¦ Experience with Paylocity, CBIZ Centrally HR, or similar payroll platforms. ¦ Experience with organizational change, leadership development, and employee-engagement initiatives. Desired Leadership Characteristics The successful candidate will be: ¦ Collaborative: Builds productive relationships across departments and organizational levels. ¦ Approachable: Creates an HR environment in which employees and managers feel comfortable seeking guidance. ¦ Strategic: Connects HR initiatives to institutional priorities and long-term organizational needs. ¦ Hands-on: Willing to personally engage in operational HR matters within a small-college environment. ¦ Solutions-oriented: Approaches complex employee and organizational issues thoughtfully and constructively. ¦ Trustworthy: Handles confidential information and sensitive situations with discretion and integrity. ¦ Mission-focused: Understands and appreciates the unique role of a women’s college and its educational mission. ¦ Change-oriented: Can help the College evolve its policies, practices, and organizational culture in response to changing workforce needs. ¦ Service-minded: Views Human Resources as both a strategic partner and a resource for the campus community. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537232399": {
      "title": "Principal Faculty Physician Assistant Program",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Principal Faculty Physician Assistant Program Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 East Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Contractor Description The Stephens College PA program is based on a 12-month didactic phase and a 15 month clinical phase that culminate in the conferral of a Master of Physician Assistant Studies. Principal faculty for the Stephens PA program will assist with the development, content delivery and assessment of didactic curriculum. The successful candidate will teach, evaluate, and advise didactic and clinical phase students. All principal faculty participate in the continuous review, analysis, and evaluation of the program’s mission. Other duties include scholarship, academic advising, service and professional activities. All principle faculty receive .20 release time to pursue clinical practice and/or research. This is a 12-month, regular, full-time, non-tenure track faculty position. This is not a remote position. Responsibilities: Serve as course director or co-director as needed for program. Teach a minimum of 6 credit hours per semester. Advise students. Contribute to collecting and managing data to track learning outcomes for students and performance outcomes for the program. Proctor exams and provide remediation as necessary. Member of the SPPC committee, Curriculum committee and Admissions Committee. Assist the program director with the continuous development, review, planning, outcomes evaluation, and general effectiveness of the PA program Assist the program director with continuous program review and evaluation, curriculum revision, evaluation of course syllabi and lesson plans for the didactic curriculum. Participate college committees, and state and national organizations. Attend open houses, special events and graduation ceremonies. Coordinate, review and revise academic policies and procedures as needed. Participate in accreditation activities. Requirements Terminal degree - Masters from an ARC accredited PA program One year of clinical practice experience preferred. Teaching experience preferred. Current NCCPA certification. Eligible for licensure in the State of Missouri. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537232397": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Admissions",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Senior Director of Admissions Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Exempt FT Description The Senior Director of Admissions provides leadership for the College's undergraduate recruitment efforts, leading a high-performing admissions team responsible for attracting, enrolling, and supporting the next generation of Stephens students. This position partners closely with the Vice President for Enrollment Management to implement recruitment strategies that advance institutional enrollment goals and strengthen the student experience from first inquiry through enrollment. Position Responsibilities: I. Provide leadership for the recruitment team, ensuring staff are aligned, accountable, and equipped to execute recruitment strategies effectively. Lead, supervise, and evaluate admissions staff by setting clear performance expectations, providing ongoing coaching and feedback, conducting performance reviews, and taking appropriate corrective action as needed to support accountability, professional growth, and achievement of admissions goals. Conduct regular one-on-one meetings with recruitment staff, establish measurable performance expectations, monitor progress toward individual and team goals, and provide coaching to support success. Lead the execution of recruitment strategies and hold the recruiting team accountable for achieving inquiry, application, admit, and enrollment goals. Ensure prospective students and families receive clear, accurate, and student-centered information about tuition, fees, financial aid, scholarships, and overall cost of attendance. Ensure timely, accurate, and student-centered communication with prospective students, families, and school counselors. Support and strengthen admissions policies, procedures, and best practices to ensure consistency, equity, compliance, and operational efficiency. Train, coach, and evaluate recruitment staff on relationship-building, admissions processes, financial aid, overall cost of attendance, CRM usage, and institutional messaging. Foster a collaborative, student-centered culture focused on professionalism, accountability, responsiveness, and continuous improvement. II. Use data to monitor performance and guide recruitment efforts. Oversee the full admissions funnel, including inquiry generation, prospect cultivation, application review, admissions decisions, yield activities, and enrollment conversion. Monitor admissions data and funnel metrics to identify trends, assess recruitment effectiveness, forecast enrollment outcomes, and recommend improvements. Prepare reports and recommendations to support recruitment planning, enrollment forecasting, and continuous improvement. Support strategic enrollment planning and implementation of recruitment programs and activities, marketing plans, technology, and office goals related to the recruitment of first-time students, transfer students, international students, graduate students, and underrepresented students, as well as the review of their applications. III. Drive continuous improvement across recruitment, operations, and student experience Supervise and support recruitment staff in the management of recruitment territories, including travel, relationship development, prospect cultivation, and enrollment goal attainment. Manage a recruitment territory, including travel, relationship development, and prospect cultivation. Collaborate with the marketing and communications team on recruitment messaging, campaigns, digital outreach, and admissions materials. Plan and oversee admissions events, including open houses, campus visits, information sessions, admitted student events, virtual visit days, and enrollment days. Conduct all activities within the established guidelines of the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act, FERPA. Stay current with admissions trends, best practices, and professional standards. IV. Increase institutional visibility with internal and external collaborations. Represent the institution at recruitment events, community meetings, conferences, and partner organizations. Collaborate with academic departments and campus partners to support program-specific recruitment and enrollment goals. Requirements Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree preferred. Minimum of seven (7) years of progressive experience in admissions, enrollment management, student recruitment, higher education, or a related field. Demonstrated experience leading, supervising, coaching, and developing recruitment or admissions professionals. Strong knowledge of admissions processes, recruitment strategies, CRM systems, enrollment funnel management, and yield strategies. Demonstrated ability to develop and implement recruitment plans that support institutional enrollment goals. Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. Demonstrated ability to use data to inform decisions, improve outcomes, and guide recruitment strategy. Strong organizational, project management, problem-solving, and decision-making skills. Ability to collaborate effectively with senior leadership, academic departments, marketing, financial aid, student services, and other campus partners. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively with prospective students and families about tuition, fees, financial aid, scholarships, and overall cost of attendance. Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends and travel as needed. Ability to meet the transportation needs of the position. Preferred Qualifications Experience with Slate, Salesforce, Ellucian, or another CRM. Experience developing recruitment plans and managing yield initiatives. Experience leading admissions strategy for multiple student populations. Experience in one or more of the following: first-time student admissions, transfer admissions, graduate      admissions, international admissions, and/or performing arts admissions. Experience working in a tuition-dependent college or university environment. Experience recruiting to distinctive academic programs. Experience preparing admissions reports, enrollment projections, and recommendations for senior leadership. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537234690": {
      "title": "Campus Safety and Security Manager",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Campus Safety and Security Manager Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Base Pay $50,000.00 - $55,000.00 / Year Employee Type Exempt FT Description The Campus Safety & Security Manager serves as the operational leader of the Stephens College Campus Safety & Security team, responsible for the day-to-day oversight of personnel, patrol operations, training, and departmental compliance. Reporting directly to the Executive Director of Campus Safety, Security & Emergency Management, this role bridges frontline security operations and executive-level leadership. The Manager ensures the consistent delivery of professional, effective, and community-centered safety services while supporting the Executive Director in advancing departmental strategy, policy development, and institutional safety goals. The Campus Safety & Security Manager exemplifies the department’s commitment to a safe, secure, and welcoming campus environment for all students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Schedule: The Campus Safety & Security Manager works five (5) eight-hour shifts per week. The Manager’s schedule is adjusted to meet the operational needs of the department and may include days, evenings, overnight, weekends, and holidays. The Manager is expected to serve in a fill-in and float capacity as needed, covering vacancies, supporting staffing continuity, and responding to critical incidents or emergencies outside of their regularly assigned hours. Flexibility and availability are essential to this role. Duties/Responsibilities: I. Operational Oversight and Supervision: Oversees the daily operations of Campus Safety & Security, ensuring consistent patrol coverage, responsiveness, and professional conduct across all shifts. Directly supervises Campus Safety & Security Lead Officers, including scheduling, shift assignments, and performance monitoring. Serves as a working supervisor, conducting patrols, responding to incidents, and performing all officer duties as needed to support operational requirements. Monitors shift activities, reviews daily logs and incident reports, and ensures timely and accurate documentation of all security-related events. Manages the 24/7 scheduling function, including call-out coverage, shift adjustments, and staffing continuity plans. II. Campus Patrol and Safety Monitoring: Conducts and directs routine and directed patrols of campus grounds to deter criminal activity and maintain a visible security presence. Oversees monitoring of campus conditions, building security, residence halls, AEDs, vehicle registration, fire extinguishers, and elevator safety systems. Ensures officers conduct consistent walk-throughs and building checks in accordance with departmental protocols. III. Training and Professional Development: Develops, coordinates, and delivers training programs for all Campus Safety & Security staff, including new employee onboarding and ongoing in-service training. Ensures all officers meet and maintain required certifications and competencies in areas including emergency response, first aid/CPR/AED, de-escalation, OC, baton, handcuffing, and defense tactics. Tracks training completion, maintains training records, and reports compliance status to the Executive Director. Identifies training needs through observation, incident review, and performance evaluations, and adjusts training programs accordingly. Promotes a culture of continuous learning, professional growth, and adherence to best practices within the department. IV. Investigations, Enforcement, and Reporting: Reviews, approves, and ensures accuracy and completeness of all incident reports and documentation generated by officers. Conducts or oversees investigations of security incidents, policy violations, and abnormal campus activity. Supports Clery Act compliance reporting by ensuring accurate incident classification, timely entry, and appropriate referrals to law enforcement. Enforces and documents violations of campus parking rules, regulations, and college policies. V. Campus Services and Community Engagement: Ensures delivery of key campus services including safety escorts, access assistance, jump starts, and lost and found management. Supports victims of crime through appropriate documentation, referral to law enforcement, and connection to campus resources. Serves as a visible, approachable, and professional presence for students, faculty, staff, and visitors. VI. Collaboration and External Relations: Maintains active working relationships with Columbia Police Department, Columbia Fire Department,  and other external law enforcement and emergency response partners. Coordinates with institutional departments including Student Experience, Facilities, and Residence Life to support a collaborative approach to campus safety and security. Assists in the planning and execution of security operations for campus events, high-profile speakers, and emergency situations. VII. Executive Support and Departmental Leadership: Serves as the primary point of operational continuity, assuming responsibility for departmental functions in the absence of the Executive Director. Provides regular operational updates to the Executive Director, including staffing status, incident trends, training compliance, and departmental concerns. Assists the Executive Director in policy development, procedure review, and departmental planning initiatives. Participates in budget preparation and resource management as directed, including equipment, uniforms, and supplies. Supports emergency management planning and response efforts, serving in an assigned role within the College’s Crisis Leadership Team and Emergency Operations Plan. Contributes to accreditation, compliance, and audit processes as assigned, including Clery Act and IACLEA standards. Requirements Associate’s degree in Criminal Justice, Emergency Management, Public Safety, or a related field; or equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum of three (3) years of experience in security, law enforcement, campus safety, or a closely related field Minimum of one (1) year of supervisory or lead experience in a security or public safety environment Demonstrated knowledge of security operations, incident response, and emergency management principles Strong written and verbal communication, interpersonal, problem-solving, and customer service skills Ability to exercise sound judgment, maintain composure, and lead effectively in high-stress situations Proficiency with security technology including CCTV systems, two-way radios, access control systems, and incident reporting platforms Ability to maintain confidentiality Valid driver’s license and the ability to operate a college vehicle. Must meet and maintain Stephens      Driver Qualification and Vehicle Policy. Must be able to acquire a Missouri Class E Driver’s License. (Stephens College funded) Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Criminal Justice, Emergency Management, Organizational Leadership, or a related field Experience in higher education campus safety or security Familiarity with Clery Act compliance requirements and reporting obligations ICS/NIMS training (ICS-100, ICS-200, IS-700) or experience functioning within an Incident Command System structure IACLEA, IAHSS, CPP, or other relevant professional certification Experience with training program development and delivery Physical Requirements: Ability to walk, stand, and lift moderate weights (minimum of 50 lbs.) Willingness to work in various weather conditions Availability for varied schedule, including evenings, weekends, and holidays, commensurate with departmental need ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537234689": {
      "title": "Teacher Assistant - The Children's School at Stephens College",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Teacher Assistant - The Children's School at Stephens College Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Non Exempt FT Required Degree High school Description The Teacher Assistant provides direct support to students and helps monitor and manage student behavior in both classroom and non-classroom settings. This role may involve working one-on-one with students, supervising during transitions, and stepping in when the teacher is out of the room.  This position is 30 hours/week. Responsibilities: Provide individual or small-group assistance to students under the direction of the teacher. Supervise students during lunch, recess, and other non-instructional times. Support classroom management by monitoring student behavior and engagement. Step in to maintain classroom structure if the teacher is temporarily out of the room. Uphold and reinforce school rules and safety procedures. Perform other duties as needed to support student learning and well-being. Requirements High school diploma required Bachelor’s degree preferred. Previous experience (preferably 2+ years) working with children. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537228293": {
      "title": "Dean of the Women's College",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Dean of the Women's College Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Academic Dean, Dean of Students, Dean of Faculty Industry Higher Education, Academia Employee Type Exempt FT Manage Others Yes Description About Stephens College Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. About The Women's College The Women's College is built on a simple conviction: students become leaders by doing consequential work early, mentored by accomplished faculty, staff, and alums who believe in them. Its undergraduate and graduate programs are organized into five academic pathways that unite degree programs around the careers and lives they unlock: Healthcare & Life Sciences: Future nurses train in the campus Simulation Center and at Magnet-designated Boone Health; Women in STEM Research students conduct original research across all four years; future doctors and vets train in our popular Biology program; and graduate students in Physician Assistant Studies and Counseling prepare to care for their corners of the world. Education: Future teachers stand in front of a classroom at The Children's School at Stephens College in their very first semester, because the best way to learn to teach is to teach. Equestrian & Animal Care: At the College's renowned Equestrian Center and in the Center for Companion Animal Behavior, Equestrian and Animal Behavior students learn responsibility for lives other than their own, launching careers across the equine and animal behavior fields. Creative Arts & Design: Students in fashion, graphic design, creative writing, English, and content creation make work for real audiences: through the student-run agency Creative Ink, its award-winning magazine, Stephens Life , and in the pages of Harbinger , the student-run literary journal that has published new voices for nearly a century. Business: Six concentrations, from Entrepreneurship to Hospitality Management, for students whose ambition is to build something. A sixth pathway, Performing Arts , resides in our all-gender Conservatory for the Performing Arts. The undergraduate block schedule makes all of it possible, enabling students to engage in internships, partnerships, student-led enterprises, research, and study abroad programs during the academic year. Professional experience is woven throughout every degree rather than deferred to the end as a culmination. The next Dean of the Women's College will assume leadership in a time buoyed by new momentum across the college: a new mission, a new strategic plan in development, and institutional alignment around the very things that make Stephens one of the most distinctive colleges in the nation. About the Position The Dean of the Women's College serves as the chief academic officer of the Women's College, is a member of the Academic Leadership Team, and reports to the Vice President of Academic Affairs. The Dean provides leadership in the Women's College, overseeing all academic programs, faculty, staff, and operations within the College. The Dean is responsible for fostering a culture of academic excellence, innovation, and collaboration, advancing student recruitment, retention, and success, building meaningful partnerships with external stakeholders, and acting as an ethical steward of the College's resources, facilities, and personnel. Essential Duties & Responsibilities 1. Provide academic and administrative leadership for the Women's College and oversee all academic programs, including supporting operations, in concert with faculty and staff colleagues. 2. Oversee and facilitate curriculum development and coordination, review, and academic quality across all programs in the Women's College, including new program development and continuous review of existing programs. 3. Recruit, mentor, evaluate, and support the professional development of faculty and staff, fostering a collaborative and inclusive workplace where all voices are heard and valued. 4. Develop and manage the Women's College's budget responsibly and transparently. 5. Ensure compliance with institutional, state, and federal laws, policies, and norms. 6. Work effectively with leads in Nursing, Counseling, Education, and Physician Assistant programs to ensure program-specific accreditation standards are met or exceeded. 7. Make independent recommendations on all personnel matters within the College, including faculty appointments, promotions, and tenure. 8. Contribute to shared governance by collaborating with the Faculty Assembly and its committees; ensure compliance with its policies and procedures. 9. Collaborate with the Dean of the Conservatory for the Performing Arts and senior leadership on shared academic matters and College priorities. 10. Work collaboratively and constructively with campus offices, including Admissions, Marketing, Advancement, the Registrar, the Library, Assessment, Workforce Development, and Student Success and Engagement to recruit, retain, and support the achievement of Stephens College students. 11. Champion and support student success initiatives, including in advising, retention, career readiness, and experiential learning opportunities. 12. Build and sustain relationships with community partners, industry leaders, healthcare, and other organizations relevant to the College's programs. 13. Support interdisciplinary collaboration within the Women's College and across the institution. 14. Promote the College's programs and mission to prospective students, alumni, donors, and external stakeholders. 15. Participate in professional development to remain current with trends in higher education. Requirements Required Qualifications 1. Terminal degree from an accredited institution. 2. A strong record as a productive faculty member active in their discipline and institution(s) with eligibility for appointment at the rank of Professor. 3. At least three years of successful academic administrative experience at or above the level of department chair or program director, with demonstrated oversight of academic programs, personnel, and budgets, or equivalent experiences. 4. Demonstrated commitment to student success, including experience with recruitment, retention, and program development/innovation. 5. Proven ability to build productive relationships with faculty, staff, students, and external partners. 6. Strong communication, collaboration, and organizational skills. 7. Commitment to shared governance, transparency, and academic excellence. Preferred Qualifications 1. Experience in a women's college or other mission-driven institution. 2. Experience launching new programs, including graduate programs in the sciences, health sciences, arts, and/or humanities. 3. Experience securing external funding from federal agencies, state agencies, foundations, or other external sources. 4. Experience with accreditation relevant to health science programs, e.g., ARC-PA, CACREP, etc. 5. Experience collaborating across academic and administrative units to support enrollment management, curricular coordination, faculty achievement, and student success. 6. Familiarity with the unique opportunities and challenges of small, independent colleges. Applications should consist of: 1. A substantive cover letter describing your interest in the position and addressing your qualifications and leadership approach. 2. A current curriculum vitae (CV). 3. A list of five professional references with full contact information (references will not be contacted without the explicit permission of the candidate). Nominations and expressions of interest may be submitted confidentially to Dr. Leslie Willey at lwilley@stephens.edu . Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537228291": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor Clinical Mental Health Counseling",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Assistant/Associate Professor Clinical Mental Health Counseling Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Clinical Counseling, Academia Industry Higher Education, Professor Employee Type Exempt FT Description Stephens College invites applicants for the position of Assistant/Associate Professor of Counseling. The Stephens College Counseling Program offers the Master of Education (M.Ed.) degree with specializations in Clinical Mental Health (CMHC) and School Counseling (SC). The successful applicant’s primary responsibility will be teaching courses in the core curriculum and CMHC specialization area. While quality teaching is the primary focus, the successful applicant will be expected to maintain active research/creative activity and service agendas and will be encouraged and supported in these endeavors. The successful candidate will facilitate work and classroom environments that encourage the knowledge of, respect for, and active engagement with individuals and groups from diverse cultures and backgrounds. The Counseling program’s vision is training counselors that strive to be lifelong learners who recognize the possibility for growth in all humans, and who are leaders in the field of counseling. The mission of the Stephens College Counseling program is to prepare ethically, culturally, and clinically competent professional counselors through evidence-based curriculums that address counseling skills, theory, technique, professional counselor identity, wellness, cultural competence/social justice, and ethics. Program graduates are well prepared to work in a pluralistic society serving diverse students and clientele within and across public and private schools and mental/behavioral health systems and organizations. Graduates of the Stephens Counseling Program are eligible for licensure and/or certification as school counselors in the State of Missouri and are prepared to work with diverse clientele in schools and community agencies. Essential Duties & Responsibilities Teach courses across the counseling core curriculum and CMHC specialization area. Student advisement and mentorship. Service to the Counseling program/discipline and Stephens College. Assist Director of Counseling with responsibilities related to CACREP-accreditation as needed. Requirements Required Qualifications Earned doctorate in Counseling Education from a CACREP-accredited program. ABD considered with completion expected by August 2027. Evidence of graduate level teaching experience in counseling core curriculum and CMHC specialization areas. Content and working knowledge of CACREP standards and process. Record of, or documented potential for, successful research/creative activity and publication at the state, regional, national, and/or international level. Preferred Qualifications Evidence of 2-3 years of successful graduate level teaching experience in counseling core and CMHC area. Minimum of 2-3 years of successful experience as a licensed counselor and supervisor. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Founded in 1833, Stephens College is the second-oldest women's college in the United States, dedicated to providing innovative, experiential, and career-focused education across various disciplines. Located in Columbia, Missouri, Stephens College continues to be a leader in women's education, fostering a community where students are empowered to lead, innovate, and excel. The college also provides flexible, co-educational master’s degrees and certificates for working professionals, and is home to the co-educational Conservatory for the Performing Arts at Stephens College. Ranked (over and over again) as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: big-time college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a high-energy arts community, amazing recreation, and a great downtown with lots of places to eat, shop, snack and check out the latest bands. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including our own Children’s School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537227372": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Admissions",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Senior Director of Admissions Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Exempt FT Description The Senior Director of Admissions provides leadership for the College's undergraduate recruitment efforts, leading a high-performing admissions team responsible for attracting, enrolling, and supporting the next generation of Stephens students. This position partners closely with the Vice President for Enrollment Management to implement recruitment strategies that advance institutional enrollment goals and strengthen the student experience from first inquiry through enrollment. Position Responsibilities: I. Provide leadership for the recruitment team, ensuring staff are aligned, accountable, and equipped to execute recruitment strategies effectively. Lead, supervise, and evaluate admissions staff by setting clear performance expectations, providing ongoing coaching and feedback, conducting performance reviews, and taking appropriate corrective action as needed to support accountability, professional growth, and achievement of admissions goals. Conduct regular one-on-one meetings with recruitment staff, establish measurable performance expectations, monitor progress toward individual and team goals, and provide coaching to support success. Lead the execution of recruitment strategies and hold the recruiting team accountable for achieving inquiry, application, admit, and enrollment goals. Ensure prospective students and families receive clear, accurate, and student-centered information about tuition, fees, financial aid, scholarships, and overall cost of attendance. Ensure timely, accurate, and student-centered communication with prospective students, families, and school counselors. Support and strengthen admissions policies, procedures, and best practices to ensure consistency, equity, compliance, and operational efficiency. Train, coach, and evaluate recruitment staff on relationship-building, admissions processes, financial aid, overall cost of attendance, CRM usage, and institutional messaging. Foster a collaborative, student-centered culture focused on professionalism, accountability, responsiveness, and continuous improvement. II. Use data to monitor performance and guide recruitment efforts. Oversee the full admissions funnel, including inquiry generation, prospect cultivation, application review, admissions decisions, yield activities, and enrollment conversion. Monitor admissions data and funnel metrics to identify trends, assess recruitment effectiveness, forecast enrollment outcomes, and recommend improvements. Prepare reports and recommendations to support recruitment planning, enrollment forecasting, and continuous improvement. Support strategic enrollment planning and implementation of recruitment programs and activities, marketing plans, technology, and office goals related to the recruitment of first-time students, transfer students, international students, graduate students, and underrepresented students, as well as the review of their applications. III. Drive continuous improvement across recruitment, operations, and student experience Supervise and support recruitment staff in the management of recruitment territories, including travel, relationship development, prospect cultivation, and enrollment goal attainment. Manage a recruitment territory, including travel, relationship development, and prospect cultivation. Collaborate with the marketing and communications team on recruitment messaging, campaigns, digital outreach, and admissions materials. Plan and oversee admissions events, including open houses, campus visits, information sessions, admitted student events, virtual visit days, and enrollment days. Conduct all activities within the established guidelines of the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act, FERPA. Stay current with admissions trends, best practices, and professional standards. IV. Increase institutional visibility with internal and external collaborations. Represent the institution at recruitment events, community meetings, conferences, and partner organizations. Collaborate with academic departments and campus partners to support program-specific recruitment and enrollment goals. Requirements Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree preferred. Minimum of seven (7) years of progressive experience in admissions, enrollment management, student recruitment, higher education, or a related field. Demonstrated experience leading, supervising, coaching, and developing recruitment or admissions professionals. Strong knowledge of admissions processes, recruitment strategies, CRM systems, enrollment funnel management, and yield strategies. Demonstrated ability to develop and implement recruitment plans that support institutional enrollment goals. Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. Demonstrated ability to use data to inform decisions, improve outcomes, and guide recruitment strategy. Strong organizational, project management, problem-solving, and decision-making skills. Ability to collaborate effectively with senior leadership, academic departments, marketing, financial aid, student services, and other campus partners. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively with prospective students and families about tuition, fees, financial aid, scholarships, and overall cost of attendance. Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends and travel as needed. Ability to meet the transportation needs of the position. Preferred Qualifications Experience with Slate, Salesforce, Ellucian, or another CRM. Experience developing recruitment plans and managing yield initiatives. Experience leading admissions strategy for multiple student populations. Experience in one or more of the following: first-time student admissions, transfer admissions, graduate      admissions, international admissions, and/or performing arts admissions. Experience working in a tuition-dependent college or university environment. Experience recruiting to distinctive academic programs. Experience preparing admissions reports, enrollment projections, and recommendations for senior leadership. ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.*** Summary Founded in 1833, Stephens College is the second-oldest women's college in the United States, dedicated to providing innovative, experiential, and career-focused education across various disciplines. Located in Columbia, Missouri, Stephens College continues to be a leader in women's education, fostering a community where students are empowered to lead, innovate, and excel. The college also provides flexible, co-educational master’s degrees and certificates for working professionals, and is home to the co-educational Conservatory for the Performing Arts at Stephens College. Ranked (over and over again) as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: big-time college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a high-energy arts community, amazing recreation, and a great downtown with lots of places to eat, shop, snack and check out the latest bands. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including our own Children’s School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Full-time staff work 36 hours and are paid for 40 !! Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537227367": {
      "title": "General Maintenance I",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in General Maintenance I Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Non Exempt FT Description The General Maintenance I position supports the safe, efficient, and attractive operation of Stephens College by performing preventive maintenance, repairs, and general maintenance throughout campus facilities. This position works in academic buildings, residence halls, administrative offices, athletic facilities, and other campus spaces to ensure buildings and equipment are well maintained and ready to support students, faculty, staff, and visitors. The ideal candidate is a dependable, service-oriented professional who enjoys solving problems, working with their hands, and contributing to a positive campus environment. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Perform preventive maintenance and general repairs to campus buildings, equipment, and building systems. Complete maintenance work orders in a timely and professional manner while providing excellent customer service. Perform basic repairs involving carpentry, plumbing, electrical, painting, drywall, flooring, doors, ceilings, windows, locks, changing filters and building hardware. Assist with troubleshooting and repairing HVAC equipment, and building systems as needed. Install, repair, and replace fixtures, shelving, whiteboards, signage, furniture, and other building components. Conduct routine inspections to identify maintenance concerns and recommend corrective actions. Support residence hall maintenance, including room turnovers, repairs, and preparation for student move-in and move-out. Assist with campus events by setting up and taking down tables, chairs, staging, tents, and other event equipment. Assist with office relocations, furniture moves, and departmental requests. Perform seasonal duties such as snow and ice removal, storm cleanup, and exterior maintenance. Maintain tools, equipment, and assigned work areas in a safe and organized manner. Accurately document completed work using the Facilities ticketing system. Follow all College policies and procedures, including OSHA safety standards and proper use of personal protective equipment (PPE). Work collaboratively with other Facilities team members and departments to meet campus needs. Participate in rotating on-call schedule for emergency response and after-hours call-in situations when required. Perform other duties as assigned. Requirements Required High school diploma or equivalent. One to three years of general building maintenance, construction, or related experience. Working knowledge of carpentry, plumbing, electrical, painting, and general building maintenance. Ability to safely operate hand tools, power tools, ladders, lifts, and maintenance equipment. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills. Excellent communication and customer service skills. Ability to work independently while contributing to a team environment. Valid driver's license with an acceptable driving record. Preferred Experience in commercial, institutional, or higher education facilities maintenance. Experience using computerized maintenance management systems. Knowledge of HVAC systems and preventive maintenance practices. Physical Requirements Frequently lift, carry, push, and pull up to 50 pounds; occasionally lift up to 75 pounds with assistance. Stand, walk, bend, kneel, climb, crawl, and work from ladders or lifts for extended periods. Work indoors and outdoors in varying weather conditions. Safely use maintenance equipment, power tools, and motorized vehicles. Ability to respond to emergency maintenance situations outside normal work hours when necessary. Summary Founded in 1833, Stephens College is the second-oldest women's college in the United States, dedicated to providing innovative, experiential, and career-focused education across various disciplines. Located in Columbia, Missouri, Stephens College continues to be a leader in women's education, fostering a community where students are empowered to lead, innovate, and excel. The college also provides flexible, co-educational master’s degrees and certificates for working professionals, and is home to the co-educational Conservatory for the Performing Arts at Stephens College. Ranked (over and over again) as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: big-time college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a high-energy arts community, amazing recreation, and a great downtown with lots of places to eat, shop, snack and check out the latest bands. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including our own Children’s School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Full-time staff work 36 hours and are paid for 40 !! Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537225868": {
      "title": "Safety & Security Officer",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Safety & Security Officer Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Base Pay $19.00 / Hour Job Category Campus Security, Security Officer Industry Security Employee Type Non Exempt FT Description The Stephens College Campus Safety & Security Officer is a vital member of the Campus Safety team, entrusted with maintaining a safe, secure, and welcoming environment for students, faculty, staff, and visitors. This position is responsible for conducting routine and directed patrols of campus grounds, responding to security incidents and emergencies, and enforcing college policies and safety protocols in support of a thriving campus community. The Campus Safety & Security Officer upholds the safety and well-being of all members of the college community in alignment with institutional values and public safety best practices. Schedule: Safety & Security Officers’ work set, non-rotating schedules across our 24/7/365 operation. Currently, officers work 5 eight-hour shifts covering Days, Swing, and Overnight. Some schedule flexibility may be required based on the needs of the College. Shift differentials are provided for second shift ($0.50 per hour additional) and third shift ($1.00 per hour additional). Requirements I. Campus Patrol and Safety Monitoring: Proactively patrols campus property-deters criminal activity and maintains visibility. Observes and monitors campus conditions. Verifies the security of all campus buildings to include dorms by consistent walk-throughs. Monitors AEDs, vehicle registration, fire extinguishers, elevator safety calls. II. Investigations and Enforcement: Enforces campus parking rules and regulations. Enforces and documents any violations of the college’s policies, rules and regulations. Investigate and document any abnormal activity. III. Campus Services: Provides key services, safety escorts, jump starts and assistance to students, staff, faculty, and visitors. Support victims of crime by documenting criminal incidents and referring to the appropriate law enforcement agency. Maintains lost and found property. IV. Collaboration Efforts: Supports all institutional departments including Student Experience and Facilities, to ensure a coordinated approach to campus safety and security. Collaborate with local law enforcement and external agencies to support crime prevention initiatives, enhance campus safety awareness, and coordinate resources when needed. V. Training and Development: Participate in ongoing training of security best practices, emergency response procedures, first aid, defense tactics, OC, baton, handcuffing, campus policies, and Campus Safety & Security protocols. Attend required training as assigned. Note: This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive. The employee may be required to perform other related duties as assigned to meet the ongoing needs of the College. Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537225865": {
      "title": "Custodial Team Member",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Custodial Team Member Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Job Category Custodian, Facilities Employee Type Non Exempt FT Description As a member of the Stephens College Custodial Team, you will provide a beautiful environment for all the people that learn, live, and work on campus. Your day may entail general cleaning in a dorm, mopping the floor in the student center, taking cardboard from receptacles in academic buildings, or helping a colleague prepare a faculty office. In a nutshell, the custodial team is to help in whatever capacity will lead to enhancing the experience of the Stephens community. Our Custodial Team at Stephens College is not your typical cleaning crew. The employees within this department work in a fast-paced, close-knit, compassionate environment. The teams are intentional about building camaraderie and participating in campus-wide events and activities. We’re always looking for team members who are committed to what they do and are dedicated to success while enhancing the lives of those around them. We are passionate about providing a fair wage, excellent benefits, and a supportive work environment where you may Learn, Grow, and Lead. In fact, that is our mission statement. Job duties will vary upon assignment but may include: Maintains the overall appearance of Stephens College buildings by: Stripping, waxing, buffing, sweeping, vacuuming and mopping floors and stairs, Cleaning and disinfecting showers, sinks, drains and grout in restrooms and locker rooms and ensuring supplies are provided, Cleaning glass doors, mirrors and windows, furniture, vents and baseboards in hallways, residence hall kitchens, partitions and displays. Miscellaneous cleaning throughout campus. Assist with snow removal as needed. Maintains custodial equipment assigned by following operating, storage and cleaning procedures and notifying supervisor of problems as they occur. Maintains communications with supervisors regarding faulty equipment or fixture deficiencies such s defective lights or plumbing. Refers work orders to supervisor when repairs are needed. Inspects building for unsafe conditions and either corrects the problem or notifies supervisor of the need to correct the problem. Lifting involved with moving furniture, cleaning out rooms and buildings. Follows safety procedures. Communicates openly and recognizes contributions of co-workers and supervisor. Maintains positive, professional working relationship with management and co-workers. Performs other duties as assigned. Requirements High school diploma or GED with 1-2 years experience in related field Ability to read and understand written instructions and product labels Ability to operate basic cleaning equipment Ability to complete work independently Ability to lift 25 lbs. without assistance, 50 lbs. with assistance We’re looking for someone who can be passionate about their job and have fun doing it, who will work with integrity, and who enjoys finding ways to support the mission of Learn. Grow. Lead. Everyday. We’ll also want to know you can demonstrate these attributes: Ability to work Monday - Friday, with weekends off. Ability to learn and thrive in a fast-paced environment Great work ethic and a desire to succeed Be a part of this amazing community of innovative thinkers, doers and dreamers. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537227349": {
      "title": "HVAC Maintenance Technician",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in HVAC Maintenance Technician Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Non Exempt FT Description The HVAC Maintenance Technician supports the safe, reliable, and efficient operation of campus facilities by performing preventive maintenance, troubleshooting, repairs, and installations related primarily to HVAC systems and secondarily to plumbing and other mechanical systems as needed. This position services academic, residential, administrative, dining, and event spaces across campus while supporting the daily operations and goals of Stephens College. The technician will work independently on assigned tasks, participate in an on-call rotation, and assist during emergencies, campus events, and peak workload periods such as residence hall turnovers. Essential Duties and Responsibilities HVAC Systems Maintain, troubleshoot, repair, and install HVAC systems including: Boilers Chillers Air handling units (AHUs) Rooftop units (RTUs) Fan coils VAV systems Split systems Heat pumps Perform seasonal startup and shutdown procedures. Replace filters, belts, motors, bearings, sensors, and other HVAC components. Diagnose airflow, temperature control, and system performance issues. Perform refrigerant leak checks and manage refrigerants in accordance with EPA guidelines. Work with Building Automation Systems (BAS) to monitor and adjust system performance. Maintain mechanical rooms and HVAC-related equipment spaces in a clean and organized condition. Mechanical and Plumbing Support Assist with plumbing repairs and maintenance including fixtures, valves, pumps, water heaters, drains, and piping. Assist with other mechanical projects as assigned, including boiler systems, commercial kitchen equipment, and campus utilities. Respond to water leaks, heating/cooling failures, and emergency maintenance situations. Work Orders and Documentation Complete preventive and corrective maintenance work orders accurately and in a timely manner. Record labor, materials used, and equipment notes in the work order system. Provide job estimates and submit purchase requests when required. Communicate equipment concerns, deficiencies, and unsafe conditions to supervisors. General Facilities Support Assist with residence hall turnovers and campus projects during peak operational periods. Support campus events, moves, shutdowns, and emergency operations as needed. Coordinate and communicate professionally with faculty, staff, students, contractors, and vendors. Maintain positive working relationships with team members across all Facilities Departments. Safety and Compliance Follow OSHA, EPA, NFPA, and campus safety procedures including: Lockout/Tagout Ladder safety Fall protection Refrigerant handling Properly operate and maintain hand tools, power tools, lifts, meters, and shop equipment. Report and/or correct unsafe conditions when identified. Additional Expectations Maintain professionalism and confidentiality. Participate in ongoing professional development and training. Support the mission and goals of Stephens College. Perform other duties as assigned. Requirements Required Skills and Abilities Knowledge of HVAC controls and mechanical systems. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills related to HVAC and mechanical systems. Ability to work independently and prioritize workload effectively. Ability to read and interpret blueprints, schematics, manuals, and technical documentation. Ability to use hand tools, power tools, meters, gauges, and diagnostic equipment. Strong communication and customer service skills. Basic computer skills including email and work order systems. Preferred Qualifications EPA 608 Certification (required or ability to obtain within 90 days). HVAC certification, journeyman license, or technical/trade school training preferred. Experience with Building Automation Systems (Trane, Johnson Controls, Siemens, etc.) preferred. Experience in commercial, institutional, or campus facilities maintenance preferred. OSHA 10 or OSHA 30 certification preferred. Education and Experience High school diploma or GED required. Technical/trade school or apprenticeship training preferred. Minimum 3 years of HVAC or mechanical maintenance experience preferred. Physical Requirements and Work Conditions Work is performed in classrooms, residence halls, mechanical rooms, rooftops, crawlspaces, offices, dining facilities, and outdoor campus environments with exposure to varying temperatures and weather conditions. This position requires: Frequent standing, walking, climbing stairs and ladders. Frequent bending, kneeling, crouching, reaching, pushing, pulling, and lifting. Frequent lifting of up to 50 pounds and occasional lifting up to 100 pounds. Occasional work in confined spaces and at elevated heights. Participation in an on-call rotation and after-hours emergency response. Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Full-time staff work 36 hours and are paid for 40 !! ***A resume and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position. *** Summary Founded in 1833, Stephens College is the second-oldest women's college in the United States, dedicated to providing innovative, experiential, and career-focused education across various disciplines. Located in Columbia, Missouri, Stephens College continues to be a leader in women's education, fostering a community where students are empowered to lead, innovate, and excel. The college also provides flexible, co-educational master’s degrees and certificates for working professionals, and is home to the co-educational Conservatory for the Performing Arts at Stephens College. Ranked (over and over again) as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: big-time college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a high-energy arts community, amazing recreation, and a great downtown with lots of places to eat, shop, snack and check out the latest bands. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including our own Children’s School ). Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537227340": {
      "title": "Facilities General Helper",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Facilities General Helper Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Non Exempt FT Description The Facilities General Helper provides general labor and support to the Facilities team in maintaining a safe, clean, functional, and attractive campus. This position assists with maintenance, grounds, custodial support, mailroom operations, event setups, moving, deliveries, and other campus needs. The ideal candidate is dependable, hardworking, safety-conscious, willing to learn, and able to work both independently and as part of a team. Essential Duties & Responsibilities: General Maintenance Support Assist general maintenance staff with routine maintenance, repairs, and campus projects. Move, load, unload, and transport furniture, equipment, supplies, and materials. Assist with event setups, move-in, move-out, deliveries, and other campus activities. Perform basic painting, patching, caulking, changing filters and other general maintenance tasks. Assist with minor repairs and preventative maintenance under the direction of general maintenance staff. Use basic hand and power tools safely after proper training. Maintain clean and organized work areas. Mailroom Support Provide backup support to the campus mailroom as needed. Receive, sort, organize, and distribute mail and packages. Load students packages and tag faculty/staff package when received. Assist with outgoing mail, shipping, and package preparation. Move and transport mail and packages throughout campus. Maintain organized mailroom and package staging areas. Handle mail and packages professionally and confidentially. Grounds and Custodial Support Assist with general campus grounds upkeep, including litter removal, weeding, mulching, planting, and seasonal work. Assist custodial staff with moving furniture, room preparation, cleaning support, and special projects. Assist with storm cleanup, snow and ice removal, and other seasonal needs. Report maintenance, safety, or facility concerns to the appropriate supervisor. On-Call Responsibilities Participate in the Facilities department’s on-call rotation and respond to after-hours facility needs and emergencies as scheduled. Follow established procedures for documenting and communicating after-hours calls, work, and follow-up needs. Safety and Customer Service Follow all Facilities safety procedures, OSHA requirements, and campus policies. Properly use PPE and operate equipment only after appropriate training. Provide courteous and professional service to students, faculty, staff, visitors, and vendors. Maintain professionalism and confidentiality when working in occupied campus spaces and handling mail or packages. Report accidents, injuries, unsafe conditions, or property damage immediately. Other Duties Assist other Facilities and Operations teams as workload and departmental needs require. Work during move-in, move-out, special events, severe weather, and other periods of increased activity. Perform other duties as assigned. Requirements High school diploma or equivalent preferred. Previous experience in maintenance, construction, landscaping, custodial work, general labor, moving, or mail/package handling preferred but not required. Ability to follow written and verbal instructions. Ability to safely use basic tools and equipment after training. Ability to lift, carry, push, and pull materials and equipment up to 50 pounds . Ability to work indoors and outdoors in varying weather conditions. Valid driver’s license; Class E preferred. Reliable attendance and punctuality. Ability and willingness to participate in the department’s on-call rotation. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Dependable and hardworking with a strong work ethic. Willingness to learn and assist in multiple areas of Facilities and Operations. Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Good communication and customer service skills. Ability to prioritize tasks and respond to changing needs. Ability to maintain professionalism and confidentiality when handling mail, packages, and working in occupied campus spaces. Physical Requirements Frequent standing, walking, bending, kneeling, reaching, and climbing stairs. Ability to lift and carry materials and equipment up to 50 pounds , with or without reasonable accommodation. Ability to work indoors and outdoors in varying weather conditions. May occasionally work in confined, elevated, or physically demanding areas. May require evening, weekend, holiday, or after-hours work as part of the on-call rotation. Work Environment This position works throughout campus, including academic buildings, offices, residence halls, shops, mechanical spaces, the mailroom, and outdoor areas. The position may be exposed to noise, dust, chemicals, machinery, and varying weather conditions. The Facilities General Laborer/Helper is expected to contribute to a team-oriented, service-focused, and safety-conscious work environment and assist wherever departmental needs require. Summary Stephens College began in 1833 with a belief that women deserved access to the power of education. Nearly two centuries later, that work is unfinished. Too many people who want to create, care, build, question, and act with purpose still lack the access or education that would empower them to lead. Stephens exists to widen the frame of possibility. Through personalized, experiential education rooted in care, mentorship, and professional engagement, Stephens turns learning into action and action into lasting impact. That conviction takes shape in our Women's College, as well as our Conservatory for the Performing Arts and a portfolio of graduate programs, which accept all genders. It takes shape in Columbia, Missouri, a major college town whose professional, cultural, and civic life acts as an extension of the campus. And most importantly, it takes shape in our students, who find at Stephens a community that knows their name and talents, who feel empowered to speak up and take charge of their own education, and who, in turn, shape life on campus and in Columbia. Stephens students graduate ready not just for what the world will ask of them, but ready to shape what comes next. Ranked as one of the best college towns in America, Columbia, Missouri, is home to 36,000+ college students and 118,000 residents. Columbia — conveniently located approximately 100 miles from both Kansas City and St. Louis — has it all: college sports, film, music and arts festivals, a thriving arts community, amazing recreation, and a historic downtown called The District. The Columbia community offers outstanding health care options, as well as excellent educational opportunities for every level of student (including The Children's School ). Stephens offers an attractive benefits package that includes health insurance, an Employee Assistance Program, Supplemental Retirement Annuity, Faculty/Staff Grants, tuition waivers and tuition paid undergraduate classes for dependents. Full-time staff work 36 hours and are paid for 40 !! Stephens is an Equal Opportunity Employer and will recruit and employ qualified personnel without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, or status as a veteran."
    },
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537225920": {
      "title": "Student Success Center Professional and Writing Tutor",
      "description": "CBIZ21203:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Student Success Center Professional and Writing Tutor Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 1200 E Broadway,\n    \n                      Columbia, MO, 65215,\n                      United States Employee Type Exempt FT Minimum Experience 5 Years Description The Stephens College Margaret Campbell Student Success Center announces one part-time position (15 hours weekly) for Center Professional & Academic Writing Tutor . The MCSSC seeks a professional who can be a collaborative member of the MCSSC team, provide support to students facing academic challenges, and provide writing tutoring, developing content for workshops and written communication, and supporting at-risk students. Professional and Writing Tutor (15 hours weekly) Responsibilities Reach out to current students, particularly those at risk, who may benefit from academic support with compassion and kindness and then support them in creating a plan for success. Guide students to maximize their learning experiences and connect students to other campus supports as needed. Help students recognize challenges to their persistence in college and empower them to resolve these challenges. Assist students with reading and academic skills/learning strategies for general education courses or in specific majors. Assist students with writing assignments (including research and citation). Proctor exams for students with Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) accommodations as needed. Prepare and present academic skills and writing workshops to students. Develop and write content for the Center’s Canvas course, newsletter, and/or blog posts. Communicate with faculty regarding students who are having course challenges. Maintain confidentiality of student records. Other duties as assigned. Requirements Teaching experience with diverse populations preferred Excellent written, listening, oral, and interpersonal communication skills that promote effective working relationships with diverse student populations Master’s degree preferred but will consider a graduate student with a high level of academic achievement/experience ***A professional CV/resume, cover letter, and three references are required to be uploaded when applying for this position.***"
    }
  },
  "rows": {
    "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537257859": {
      "school_id": 706,
      "posting_url": "https://secure5.entertimeonline.com/ta/CBIZ21203.careers?ShowJob=537257859",
      "job_title_in_post": "Assistant Professor of English/Creative Writing",
